    <title>America’s AI Ambitions Depend on Taiwan</title>
    <link href="https://latifkedi.github.io/ahbar/?item=2026-11-06-81-america-s-ai-ambitions-depend"/>
    <link rel="related" href="https://www.foreignaffairs.com/taiwan/americas-ai-ambitions-depend-taiwan-lin-chia-lung"/>
    <id>https://latifkedi.github.io/ahbar/item/2026-11-06-81-america-s-ai-ambitions-depend</id>
    <updated>2026-11-03T05:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;The United States and China are locked in a global competition to determine who will dominate artificial intelligence and whether it will be democracies or autocracies that set the standards and values of the new digital age. The United States leads the world in the sophistication and power of its artificial intelligence models, and its innovation base and investment in research and development remain unparalleled.But AI competition is much broader than modeling alone. It encompasses a technology ecosystem that includes power generation, servers, and deployment in real-world applications.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Source:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.foreignaffairs.com/taiwan/americas-ai-ambitions-depend-taiwan-lin-chia-lung&quot;&gt;Foreign Affairs&lt;/a&gt; · &lt;strong&gt;Tags:&lt;/strong&gt; #geopolitics #defense-strategy&lt;/p&gt;</summary>
